        "title": "Wind power",
        "text": "For centuries the power of the wind has been used to drive machines directly, for tasks such as grinding wheat or pumping water. Recently, however, the wind has joined other natural forces such as water and steam as a practical way of generating electricity.\n\nTraditional ways of generating electricity, in coal- or oil-fired power stations, have two major drawbacks: they pollute the environment, and the fuels they use are inefficient and non-renewable. As people have become more aware of the environment, there have been calls for a greener alternative. Nuclear power, although more efficient and less polluting, is seen by many people as unacceptable because of the danger of accidents like those at Chernobyl and Three Mile Island. Wind power, on the other hand, is clean and renewable and, with modern advances, surprisingly efficient.\n\nIn the 1970s Britain was in the forefront of research into wind power. In the 1980s, however, interest in the wind faded because of cheap North Sea oil, a strong pro-nuclear lobby and a pricing structure that made wind farms uneconomical to set up. Britain, the windiest country in Europe, had to wait until 1991 for its first wind farm. The farm, at Delabole in Cornwall, was originally the idea of local people who opposed plans for a nuclear power station nearby and decided to start a private company to generate power for the area from the wind. They had to fight opposition from the local council and other residents, who thought the turbines would be noisy and might interfere with television signals. In the end, after showing local officials wind farms in Denmark, they won, and today there are ten huge white wind turbines on the Delabole hills.\n\nIt is in Germany and Denmark that the greatest advances in wind power have been made. Germany alone produces half of the wind-generated electricity in Europe. Every year it adds 400 megawatts (MW) of capacity, and in 2000 alone its capacity grew by 1,669 MW. Denmark now produces 30% of its electricity from wind power, and this is predicted to rise to 50% by 2017. Both countries have encouraged this growth with \"fixed feed tariffs\", which guarantee private wind power operators a good price.\n\nThe UK is catching up, and the government has set a target of 10% of all electricity to come from renewable sources by 2017, half of this to be from wind power. The 900 wind turbines now operating generate 400 MW of electricity, and to meet the target roughly 400 MW will need to be added each year. With advances in technology this is technically possible: each turbine can now produce 400 kilowatts (kW), compared with only 70 kW at the start of the 1980s. It will, however, need help from the government, which is offering financial support and giving private power companies targets to meet.\n\nBecause many people feel that wind farms spoil the view, and also because the wind is stronger at sea, many wind farms are now being built offshore, usually a few kilometres off the coast in shallow water. They cost more to build and maintain, but they produce more electricity. Britain's first offshore farm was built in 2000 at Blyth, north of Newcastle, and it was the largest in the world until May 2001, when a farm of 20 turbines opened at Middelgrunden, off Copenhagen. There are plans to build up to 18 more in the UK by 2017, and together they will produce 800 MW of electricity a year.\n\nThe use of wind power is far less advanced in the USA. Only 5% of America's power comes from the wind, although it is estimated that this could rise to as much as 12% with no changes to the power grid. However, interest in wind power is growing. There are plans to build a huge offshore wind farm off the coast of Cape Cod on the north-east seaboard. The farm will cover more than 25 square miles, have 170 turbines and produce 420 MW, at a cost of $600 million. If it is built, it will be the second biggest wind farm in the world, after the 520 MW farm planned in Ireland."
